  <article class="section-shell" aria-labelledby="article-title">
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">Guide · Working conjecture</p>
      <h1 id="article-title">The stage, the rules, and what appears.</h1>
      <p>
        Marici is testing whether several areas of fundamental physics share a common geometric
        stage and a common calculus of composition—even when the physical objects living on that
        stage are different.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body">
      <h2>Start with a map, not a list of particles</h2>
      <p>
        Imagine a transit map. The stations are places where something special can happen: a
        process can split, two boundaries can meet, a momentum can become soft, or a smooth family
        can degenerate. The lines show which stations can be reached together and in what order.
        In Marici, this structured map is called the <strong>carrier</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The carrier is not merely a set of points. It remembers boundaries, intersections, nested
        regions, duplicated occurrences, orientations, sheets, and directions of approach. Two
        boundaries that look identical after simplification may remain distinct because they came
        from different physical occurrences.
      </p>

      <h2>A board-game set: board plus rules</h2>
      <p>
        Another way to hold the distinction is to imagine opening a board-game box. The board
        provides spaces, boundaries, adjacencies, and configurations that can meet. The rulebook
        provides legal moves, combinations, and transformations that preserve a valid position.
        In Marici, the resolved carrier is like the board and the carrier algebra is like the
        rulebook: together they form the structured stage and its legal calculus.
      </p>
      <p>
        The board alone is not enough, and rules without a board have nowhere to act. Likewise,
        the carrier is not a bare list of objects and its algebra is not a detached list of
        instructions. Different sectors can use the same board-and-rules set while bringing
        different pieces or data, choosing different physical selections, and reading different
        observables from the resulting play.
      </p>

      <h2>Two technical sketches</h2>
      <p>
        The transit-map analogy can be made more precise in two familiar technical languages.
        A system architect can view the carrier as a typed topology of interfaces, operating
        strata, and consistency constraints. A Rust programmer can view its labelled occurrences
        as types whose provenance cannot be erased without an explicit map.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/carrier-for-system-architects/">
            Carrier as a system-architecture model
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/carrier-in-rust-types/">
            Carrier through the Rust type system
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The carrier is not the algebra</h2>
      <p>
        A map alone does not tell us how to travel. We also need operations: restrict to a boundary,
        take a residue, glue local descriptions, follow a degeneration, or compare two different
        orders of doing these things. These operations—and the laws governing their
        composition—form the <strong>carrier algebra</strong>.
      </p>
      <blockquote>
        The carrier says where composition and degeneration can occur. Its algebra says how data
        may move, restrict, glue, and compare there.
      </blockquote>
      <p>
        “Algebra” here does not mean arithmetic alone. It means a calculus of allowed operations,
        including higher consistency rules. Sometimes two operations commute directly. Sometimes
        their difference is repaired by a boundary or corner term. Discovering which case holds is
        part of the research.
      </p>

      <h2>Where the associahedron fits</h2>
      <p>
        The associahedron is a famous geometric object in modern scattering theory. Its faces encode
        compatible ways for a tree-level process to factorize. It is an especially clean finite
        example of a carrier: faces are factorization channels, and their incidence relations say
        which channels can occur together.
      </p>
      <p>
        Marici's proposed carrier is broader. It retains occurrence labels, normal directions,
        support conditions, exceptional geometry, loops, and degenerations that an ordinary face
        lattice cannot see. In this picture, the associahedron is not the entire structure. It is a
        tree-level chart—or shadow—of a richer resolved carrier.
      </p>

      <h2>Different physics can live on the same stage</h2>
      <p>
        Even if two sectors share a carrier and its algebra, they need not carry the same physical
        data. What lives on the carrier is called a <strong>coefficient object</strong>. A physical
        prescription must then select a state, contour, or integration cycle, and finally a readout
        turns that selection into an observable.
      </p>
      <p>The working architecture is therefore:</p>
      <p>
        <strong>observable theory = carrier + carrier algebra + coefficient object + physical
        selection + readout.</strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        A recent cosmology comparison illustrates the distinction. The one-loop two-site “bubble”
        uses a logarithmic coefficient system built from an ordinary hyperplane arrangement. The
        one-loop three-site “triangle” uses elliptic, Gauss–Manin coefficient data. The coefficient
        systems differ sharply, while the same kinds of boundary, residue, and localization
        operations continue to organize them.
      </p>

      <h2>Why corners matter</h2>
      <p>
        The bubble example also shows why the algebra cannot be replaced by a list of rules attached
        independently to each boundary. Certain regularity conditions are not preserved one boundary
        at a time. They become compatible only when all residues meeting at a corner are combined in
        the way required by the incidence geometry.
      </p>
      <p>
        In plain language: the physics can be consistent around the intersection even when no single
        wall carries a self-contained explanation. The corner relation is part of the explanation.
      </p>

      <h2>What is—and is not—being claimed</h2>
      <p>
        Marici is not claiming that scattering, cosmology, strings, flavor, and gravity are already
        derived from one object. Nor is it claiming that every structure is secretly an
        associahedron. The narrower conjecture is that these sectors may be representations or
        partial lenses of a common resolved grammar of composition and degeneration.
      </p>
      <p>
        That conjecture is deliberately falsifiable. A sector would challenge it if a required
        physical singularity or gluing operation could not be produced from the frozen carrier and
        its predeclared calculus, and instead forced a genuinely new incidence stratum. New
        coefficient complexity alone does not count as such a failure.
      </p>

      <h2>The compact picture</h2>
      <p>
        The carrier is the structured stage. The carrier algebra is the legal and coherent calculus
        of movement on that stage. Coefficient objects are what can propagate there. Physical cycles
        or states select what is realized. Observables are the resulting readouts.
      </p>
      <p>
        The research question is whether nature's apparently different sectors are built by changing
        the last three ingredients over a surprisingly stable first pair.
      </p>
    </div>
